['Here is a simple C program that takes a username as a command line argument, runs the `id` command with the provided username, and then prints the exit status of the command:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

int run_command(const char* command) {
    // Fork to create a new process
    pid_t child = fork();

    if (child == 0) {
        // Child process - execute the command
        if (execl("/bin/id", "id", "-u", command, NULL) == -1) {
            return 1; // Command failed
        }
    } else {
        // Parent process - wait for the child to finish
        int status;
        wait(&status);

        return WEXITSTATUS(status); // Get the exit status of the child process
    }

    return 0; // Command was successful
}

int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <username>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    const char* username = argv[1];

    int status = run_command(username);

    printf("Exit status of command: %d\n", status);

    return 0;
}
```

This code uses the `fork` system call to create a new process, which executes the `id` command with the provided username. The parent process waits for the child to finish and then gets its exit status using the `wait` function. The `run_command` function returns this exit status.

To compile and run this program, you can use the following commands:

```bash
gcc id_status.c -o id_status
./id_status your_username
```

Replace `your_username` with the actual username you want to query.']

